**Read:** The Art of Insight, Symmetry and conservation, and Algebra and Trigonometry 2e's functions and rates-of-change sections. Revisit the graph reading's time-weighted example. Read the whole conservation chapter, including problems, before using the balance merely as a slogan.

For a defined stock over an interval, final stock minus initial stock equals receipts minus issues plus any internal generation minus consumption, with all terms on the same quantity basis. For total mass in an ordinary nonnuclear process, internal chemical transformation does not create or destroy total mass. A particular chemical species, usable item count, or monetary valuation may have other terms.

A rate is a change per interval or its instantaneous idealization. A steady flow is not the same as zero flow; it means the relevant inventory is not changing over the chosen description. A snapshot with equal inflow and outflow does not prove steady operation at all earlier times.

**Work:** start a tank at 120 L. Supply 12 L/min for 10 min while withdrawing 7 L/min, then stop supply and withdraw 7 L/min for another 5 min. The predicted volumes are 170 L and 135 L, provided the tank capacity and outlet conditions permit those flows. Plot stock and rates separately and show where physical constraints would invalidate this account.

For a warehouse, distinguish received, inspected, available, reserved, and shipped counts. Transfer to another organization may change custody without changing the total physical stock within a larger boundary. Draw both boundaries and reconcile the two accounts. This same habit will later prevent counting a recycled stream as fresh material each time it crosses an internal pipe.

**Continue:** [Mass and component balances — account for every stream](Mass-and-component-balances.lingua#r_163GVDJGRJ1DD4JT2398GM371H).

